1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,137
James: Ava, every building starts the same way, doesn't it?

2
00:00:04,417 --> 00:00:10,843
Ava: With a sentence. A clinic with six exam rooms. A twenty-megawatt AI factory.

3
00:00:11,123 --> 00:00:16,113
James: And then weeks of drawings, before anyone knows if it actually works.

4
00:00:16,393 --> 00:00:20,677
Ava: So we asked ourselves... what if the sentence was enough?

5
00:00:23,250 --> 00:00:25,757
James: This is Floorplan Intelligence.

6
00:00:26,037 --> 00:00:28,806
Ava: Brief in. Building out.

7
00:00:29,807 --> 00:00:35,292
Ava: Now, James, the obvious move is to just ask an AI to draw the plan, right?

8
00:00:35,572 --> 00:00:46,413
James: We tried that. It draws one, very confidently. Rooms overlap. The corridor comes out ten times too wide. And then it tells you it's code-compliant.

9
00:00:46,693 --> 00:00:48,887
Ava: And a constraint solver?

10
00:00:49,167 --> 00:00:54,966
James: The opposite. Flawless geometry. But it can't read a single word of your brief.

11
00:00:55,967 --> 00:01:00,486
Ava: So we split the job, by what each tool is genuinely good at.

12
00:01:00,766 --> 00:01:09,125
James: NVIDIA Nemotron reads the intent. A solver makes the geometry exact. A rule checker cites what passed.

13
00:01:09,405 --> 00:01:13,846
Ava: And the browser lets anyone walk it, stress it, and price it.

14
00:01:14,126 --> 00:01:15,771
James: The model proposes.

15
00:01:16,051 --> 00:01:17,775
Ava: The solver proves.

16
00:01:18,055 --> 00:01:20,642
James: And the simulator tells you if it works.

17
00:01:21,641 --> 00:01:25,429
Ava: Let's start with the model. James, why Nemotron?

18
00:01:25,709 --> 00:01:40,704
James: Nemotron Nano. Nine billion parameters, and it's a hybrid: mostly Mamba-2 state-space layers, which read long context in linear time, with attention only where it matters. Small enough to run on a laptop.

19
00:01:40,984 --> 00:01:43,256
Ava: And you didn't just use it off the shelf.

20
00:01:43,536 --> 00:01:54,377
James: No. Two stages. First, Q-LoRA fine-tuning, on four hundred solver-certified clinic layouts, written in a compact plan language we designed for it.

21
00:01:54,657 --> 00:01:58,210
Ava: Then reinforcement learning, with GRPO.

22
00:01:58,490 --> 00:02:10,846
James: Right, and with no human preference model. Four rewards, computed straight from the plan. Does it parse? Do rooms overlap? Can you reach every room? Does it meet the code?

23
00:02:11,126 --> 00:02:14,208
Ava: So the plan itself grades the model.

24
00:02:15,209 --> 00:02:19,728
Ava: And when it generates, there's a grammar running inside llama-C-P-P.

25
00:02:20,008 --> 00:02:25,885
James: Which means every token it writes has to be legal. Every single line parses.

26
00:02:26,165 --> 00:02:28,595
Ava: But then you tested it properly.

27
00:02:28,875 --> 00:02:43,555
James: We did, and here's the honest part. The room program was right, every time: which rooms, and how many. The coordinates were not. A corridor of eighteen hundred millimetres came out at eighteen thousand.

28
00:02:43,835 --> 00:02:48,355
Ava: So the model writes the program, and the geometry goes to a solver.

29
00:02:49,354 --> 00:02:51,862
James: Ava, walk us through the solver.

30
00:02:52,142 --> 00:03:08,181
Ava: Google OR-Tools CP-SAT, on a hundred-millimetre grid. Every room becomes integer variables. No two rooms can overlap, by construction. Locked rooms stay exactly where you put them, and rooms joined by a door must share a wall.

31
00:03:08,461 --> 00:03:09,768
James: And everything else?

32
00:03:10,048 --> 00:03:13,757
Ava: Moves as little as possible. That's the objective.

33
00:03:14,037 --> 00:03:16,466
James: And when you ask for something impossible?

34
00:03:16,746 --> 00:03:25,262
Ava: It doesn't make something up. It returns a proof: the smallest set of rooms that can't all fit, and the arithmetic why.

35
00:03:26,263 --> 00:03:28,378
Ava: And none of this is a mock-up.

36
00:03:28,658 --> 00:03:42,451
James: Two apps on one pipeline. About twenty-two thousand lines of TypeScript, five and a half thousand of Python, and two hundred and ten tests, including golden files that freeze the simulator's numbers to the minute.

37
00:03:42,731 --> 00:03:45,735
Ava: Running in the browser, on WebGPU.

38
00:03:46,736 --> 00:03:54,128
Ava: I'm describing a primary-care clinic. Six exam rooms, a nurse station, an accessible restroom. Generate.

39
00:03:54,408 --> 00:04:00,521
James: Three candidates, ranked by the checks they pass. Nine out of nine, all three.

40
00:04:01,521 --> 00:04:03,715
James: Open one, and it's a building.

41
00:04:03,995 --> 00:04:12,667
Ava: Rendered live, in the browser. Click a room and it tells you what it's for. Or take the guided tour, in the order a patient walks it.

42
00:04:21,988 --> 00:04:26,115
James: Now, the real question. Does it cope with flu season?

43
00:04:26,395 --> 00:04:33,004
Ava: Flu-season Monday. Ninety patients, and a morning rush. Run twenty days.

44
00:04:33,284 --> 00:04:37,333
James: And there's the verdict, in plain words, with the bottleneck.

45
00:04:38,333 --> 00:04:40,135
Ava: What if we add a nurse?

46
00:04:40,415 --> 00:04:45,326
James: The assistant changes the day, runs it again, and comes back with the new wait.

47
00:04:45,606 --> 00:04:50,935
Ava: And it never guesses. Every number comes from the same engine you see on screen.

48
00:04:54,900 --> 00:05:02,135
Ava: And edits are just plain language. Make the waiting room twenty percent bigger, and keep the restrooms where they are.

49
00:05:02,415 --> 00:05:05,968
James: The solver keeps what you asked for, and moves the rest.

50
00:05:06,248 --> 00:05:09,017
Ava: And if I ask for something impossible?

51
00:05:09,297 --> 00:05:11,335
James: It tells you exactly why.

52
00:05:21,333 --> 00:05:27,445
James: Now, this is my favourite part. Same model. Same solver. Same viewer...

53
00:05:27,725 --> 00:05:30,311
Ava: ...and a completely different building.

54
00:05:31,312 --> 00:05:37,920
James: A twenty-megawatt AI factory. Liquid cooled. N plus one cooling, two-N power.

55
00:05:38,200 --> 00:05:46,168
Ava: Three designs: liquid, hybrid, and air. Every rack, cooler, UPS and generator, placed.

56
00:05:46,448 --> 00:05:50,889
James: And CP-SAT proves the layout, in about thirty milliseconds.

57
00:05:51,169 --> 00:06:00,965
Ava: Then four lenses, each answering one question. Where does the power go? Where does it get hot? And what keeps running if something breaks?

58
00:06:01,965 --> 00:06:05,596
James: So, let's break it. The grid goes down.

59
00:06:05,876 --> 00:06:07,913
Ava: And the generators take over.

60
00:06:08,193 --> 00:06:15,037
James: Worst day, then. Grid down, a generator fails, and it's forty-two degrees outside.

61
00:06:15,317 --> 00:06:19,288
Ava: And you can see exactly which racks go dark, and why.

62
00:06:25,365 --> 00:06:27,559
Ava: And what does all of this cost?

63
00:06:27,839 --> 00:06:34,526
James: To build, and to run. Where every kilowatt-hour goes. Dollars per GPU-hour.

64
00:06:34,806 --> 00:06:38,437
Ava: Change the electricity price, and everything updates.

65
00:06:41,432 --> 00:06:44,749
Ava: So, James. Does it actually hold up?

66
00:06:45,029 --> 00:06:54,982
James: Twenty out of twenty test briefs accepted, with every check passing. Five out of five impossible requests answered with a proof, not a plan.

67
00:06:55,262 --> 00:07:01,871
Ava: And it all runs on a laptop. A five-and-a-half-gigabyte model, and no data leaves the machine.

68
00:07:02,151 --> 00:07:04,110
James: And no cost per plan.

69
00:07:05,110 --> 00:07:06,755
Ava: So who's it for?

70
00:07:07,035 --> 00:07:11,163
James: Health systems, testing a clinic before they sign the lease.

71
00:07:11,443 --> 00:07:16,040
Ava: Architects, with a checked first plan in minutes, instead of days.

72
00:07:16,320 --> 00:07:23,243
James: And AI infrastructure teams, comparing liquid against air, and seeing what survives a heat wave.

73
00:07:23,523 --> 00:07:31,725
Ava: And the next building type is a config pack, not a new product. Schools. Offices. Warehouses. Labs.

74
00:07:32,726 --> 00:07:34,032
James: From a sentence...

75
00:07:34,312 --> 00:07:37,943
Ava: ...to a building you can walk, stress, and price.

76
00:07:38,223 --> 00:07:40,730
James: This is Floorplan Intelligence.

77
00:07:41,010 --> 00:07:46,261
Ava: Built for the Nebius and NVIDIA Global AI Hackathon. Thank you.

